import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const markowitzCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'corr', label: 'correlation between assets', min: -0.5, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.3 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'single-asset volatility (both)', expr: '20' },
    { label: '50/50 portfolio volatility', expr: 'sqrt((20^2 + 20^2 + 2*corr*20*20))/2' },
    { label: 'risk reduction achieved', expr: '100 - (sqrt((20^2 + 20^2 + 2*corr*20*20))/2)/20*100', unit: '%' },
  ],
};

const mptQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'Stocks (volatility 16%) and bonds (6%) with correlation 0.1 are mixed 60/40. The portfolio variance is 0.36×16² + 0.16×6² + 2×0.6×0.4×0.1×16×6. What is its volatility?',
      choices: ['Exactly 12%', 'About 10.1% — below the weighted average of the components', 'About 15%', 'About 6%'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Variance = 92.16 + 5.76 + 4.61 ≈ 102.5, so volatility ≈ 10.1% — lower than either naive blend of the inputs. The covariance term is where diversification lives (or dies): it is the only term correlation controls.',
    },
    {
      q: 'CAPM says an asset with beta 1.2, risk-free rate 4% and equity risk premium 5% should return:',
      choices: ['4% + 1.2 = 5.2%', '4% + 1.2 × 5% = 10%', '1.2 × 5% = 6%', '4% + 5% = 9%'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'E(R) = rf + β × ERP = 4 + 1.2 × 5 = 10%. Only systematic risk (beta) is priced; the rest of the asset\'s volatility can be diversified away and earns nothing extra.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does Markowitz optimisation suffer from "error maximisation" (Michaud)?',
      choices: ['It is computationally slow', 'It overweights assets whose expected returns were measured too high and covariances measured too low — the errors concentrate exactly where the weights go', 'It ignores correlations', 'It needs too little data'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The optimiser loads up on whatever looked best in the sample, so estimation noise is amplified rather than averaged. Robust estimation, Bayesian shrinkage (Black–Litterman) and even simple 1/n allocations often beat optimised portfolios out of sample.',
    },
    {
      q: 'An investor holds 500 randomly selected stocks. Which risk remains?',
      choices: ['None — all risk is diversified away', 'Systematic (market) risk; idiosyncratic risk is diversified away but the market factor cannot be', 'Only idiosyncratic risk', 'Only currency risk'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Diversification cancels firm-specific noise (falling roughly as 1/√n) but every stock still loads on common factors — recessions and rate shocks move all 500 together. That residual, systematic risk is what CAPM prices.',
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/portfolio-theory-markowitz">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Before 1952, investment advice was a list of good assets. Markowitz's <em>Portfolio
    Selection</em> changed the object of analysis: <strong>the portfolio, not the asset</strong> —
    because risk depends not just on how each holding moves but on how the holdings move
    <em>together</em>. Combine two assets imperfectly correlated and you get a mixture whose
    volatility is lower than the weighted average of its parts: risk destroyed by arithmetic, no
    forecast required. Chart every combination, take the upper edge, and you have the
    <strong>efficient frontier</strong> — the maximum expected return available at each level of
    risk. The insight won the 1990 Nobel and became the skeleton of modern asset management.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the correlation dial — watch the free lunch shrink as assets move together" widget="sliderLab" config={markowitzCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the dial carefully. Two assets, each with 20% volatility: at correlation 0.3, the 50/50
    mix runs about 16.1% — a 19% risk reduction from mixing alone; at zero correlation it falls
    to 14.1% (29% cut); at perfect correlation the benefit is exactly zero, and below zero it
    grows fast. This one identity is the entire argument for mixing bonds with equities, home
    market with international, and every "uncorrelated return stream" pitch you will ever hear —
    and also for the suspicion they deserve, since the benefit is priced in the correlation,
    and correlations move when it matters (rising toward 1 in crises, the "diversification when
    you need it least" problem).
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring return and risk: the mean-variance frame</h2>
  <p>
    Markowitz's formalisation rests on two statistics. <strong>Expected return</strong> is the
    probability-weighted average of outcomes; <strong>risk</strong> is variance (standard
    deviation) of returns — the choice of variance as the risk measure was the modelling
    decision that made everything solvable, and its limits (symmetry: a "risk" measure that
    penalises pleasant upside identically with downside; fat tails understated) are permanent
    footnotes and permanent research. The two-asset portfolio variance is the identity worth
    memorising, because every statement above is a special case of it:
  </p>
  <p>
    σ<sub>p</sub>² = w<sub>1</sub>²σ<sub>1</sub>² + w<sub>2</sub>²σ<sub>2</sub>² +
    2w<sub>1</sub>w<sub>2</sub>ρ<sub>12</sub>σ<sub>1</sub>σ<sub>2</sub>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example:</strong> a classic 60/40 stocks–bonds portfolio. Equities: 16%
    volatility; bonds: 6%; correlation 0.1. Variance = 0.36 × 16² + 0.16 × 6² + 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 ×
    0.1 × 16 × 6 = 92.16 + 5.76 + 4.61 ≈ 102.5, so σ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 10.1% — comfortably below
    the weighted "average" of the parts (0.6 × 16 + 0.4 × 6 = 12%). The covariance term — the
    only place correlation enters — is where the 2 points of risk went. Extend to n assets and
    the same structure holds: variance is a weighted sum of every pairwise covariance, and as n
    grows, the average covariance dominates — which is why <strong>diversification kills
    idiosyncratic risk (falling roughly as 1/√n) but cannot touch systematic risk</strong>: 500
    stocks still fall together in a recession, because what they share cannot be diversified
    within the asset class.
  </p>

  <h2>The efficient frontier and two-fund separation</h2>
  <p>
    With the statistics fixed, the mathematics is constrained optimisation: minimise portfolio
    variance subject to a target expected return (or maximise return subject to a variance
    budget), weights summing to one. Sweeping the target traces the <strong>efficient
    frontier</strong> — every portfolio below it is dominated (someone else has higher return at
    equal risk, or equal return at lower risk). Two structural results follow.
    <strong>The global minimum-variance portfolio</strong> anchors the curve's left tip.
    <strong>Tobin's two-fund separation</strong> adds the risk-free asset: every efficient
    portfolio is a mix of the risk-free asset and <em>one</em> optimal risky portfolio (the
    tangency portfolio, where the line from the risk-free rate touches the frontier) — meaning
    investors of every appetite need just two funds and a slider. Separation is the theoretical
    foundation of index-fund investing: if everyone's optimal risky portfolio is the same
    broadly diversified one, the advice "hold the market, adjust your cash/bond mix to taste"
    is not conservatism but arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "diversification eliminates risk." It eliminates the
    <em>avoidable</em> risk — the firm-specific noise that averages away — and leaves exactly the
    risk that is common to everything you hold. The converse misconception ("low risk assets
    always earn less") is also wrong as stated: the relationship runs through the
    <em>pricing</em> of systematic risk, and assets can be cheap or expensive on their own risk
    — the low-volatility anomaly (defensive stocks historically earning competitive returns with
    less risk) is the standing empirical embarrassment to both.
  </p>

  <h2>From Markowitz to CAPM</h2>
  <p>
    The framework's first great child is the <strong>Capital Asset Pricing Model</strong>
    (Sharpe, Lintner, Mossin — the 1960s). Equilibrium reasoning: if everyone holds the tangency
    portfolio, it must be the market portfolio; an asset's expected return then depends only on
    how much systematic risk it contributes — its <strong>beta</strong> (covariance with the
    market, normalised). E(R) = R<sub>f</sub> + β × ERP. <strong>Worked example:</strong> the
    risk-free rate is 4%, the equity risk premium 5%; a stock with β = 1.2 should earn
    4 + 1.2 × 5 = 10%. Beta 0.5 → 6.5%; beta 2 → 14%. Only covariance with the market is priced;
    an asset's own volatility is irrelevant to its expected return because it can be diversified
    away — CAPM is Markowitz plus equilibrium, and the security market line it draws is the
    default benchmark for performance attribution (alpha = return above the line). The empirical
    record is mixed in ways that built modern finance: beta explains less of cross-sectional
    return than the model hopes (the low-beta anomaly again), and the residuals have structure —
    which is precisely why <strong>factor models</strong> (Fama-French's size and value, momentum,
    quality) exist: CAPM's single factor expanded to a family, with each factor a priced
    characteristic, not a refutation of the mean-variance frame but its elaboration.
  </p>

  <h2>The estimation problem: where the theory meets its limits</h2>
  <p>
    Mean-variance optimisation needs <strong>expected returns</strong> (unobservable — you must
    forecast), <strong>variances</strong> (estimable but time-varying — volatility clustering
    means yesterday's variance is a poor but better-than-nothing proxy) and
    <strong>correlations</strong> (unstable, and historically prone to converge toward one in
    crises). The notorious consequence is Michaud's <strong>error maximisation</strong>: the
    optimiser loads maximum weight on whatever asset happened to be measured with the most
    favourable error, so unconstrained optimised portfolios routinely underperform simple
    allocations out of sample. The practical repairs define institutional practice:
    constraints (weight limits, turnover limits), <strong>shrinkage</strong> (pull estimates
    toward structure — Ledoit-Wolf for covariances), <strong>Black–Litterman</strong> (blend
    market-equilibrium expected returns with house views, in a Bayesian frame that keeps views
    from dominating), <strong>resampling</strong>, and the humbling empirical result of
    DeMiguel and colleagues that the naive <strong>1/n portfolio</strong> is hard to beat. The
    2008–2020 era added the deepest caveat: correlations and volatilities are regime-dependent,
    so the frontier is itself a forecast — risk parity, volatility targeting and factor investing
    are all attempts to manage the estimation problem rather than assume it away.
  </p>

  <h2>Risk beyond variance: drawdown, VaR and CVaR</h2>
  <p>
    Variance's symmetry — penalising upside as it penalises downside — is its practical
    limitation, and the alternatives define modern risk practice. <strong>Value at Risk
    (VaR)</strong>: the loss threshold not exceeded with probability p over horizon h (the
    5% one-day VaR of a portfolio) — banking regulation's native measure, with well-known
    pathologies: it says nothing about losses beyond the threshold (the tail is where ruin
    lives), it is not subadditive (merged portfolios can show more VaR than their parts — an
    incoherence), and it invites tail-blindness. <strong>Expected Shortfall (CVaR)</strong>:
    the average loss in the tail beyond VaR — coherent, subadditive, and the post-crisis
    Basel standard for market risk. <strong>Maximum drawdown</strong> (largest peak-to-trough
    decline) captures the lived experience investors actually abandon portfolios over — and
    the behavioural literature confirms the abandonment: returns are path-dependent in
    decision reality, and the investor who flees at the bottom converts paper loss into
    permanent loss. <strong>Worked illustration:</strong> two strategies with identical 10%
    volatility and mean return — one with small frequent losses, one with a single −35%
    drawdown — are indistinguishable to variance and separated cleanly by drawdown and CVaR;
    the investor's actual survival differs. The mean-variance frame remains the solvable
    core; risk's measurement pluralism is where practice has outgrown the textbook.
  </p>

  <h2>Factor investing: the elaborated frontier</h2>
  <p>
    The anomalies that embarrassed CAPM became an industry. <strong>Size</strong> (small caps
    out-earning beta predictions — Banz's finding), <strong>value</strong> (cheap-by-fundamentals
    outperforms growth — Fama-French's HML factor), <strong>momentum</strong>
    (Jegadeesh-Titman: past winners persist for months), <strong>quality and
    profitability</strong>, <strong>low volatility</strong> — factors priced beyond beta,
    persistent across markets, and contested in interpretation: risk compensation (factors
    are priced because they load on crash states — value crashed in the tech bubble) or
    behaviour (mispricing structured by limits to arbitrage). The portfolio-theory
    translation: the frontier's dimensions multiplied — a multi-factor covariance structure
    where each factor is a priced direction, and "diversification" means factor
    diversification, not just security count. The practical record: factor premia attenuate
    after publication (the publication-decay effect is real and measurable), factor timing is
    hard, and the honest institutional answer is broad, cheap, rebalanced exposure — the
    frontier's logic applied to itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The n-asset mathematics.</strong> The frontier is the solution of a quadratic
    programme; in matrix form, the minimum-variance weights are w ∝ Σ<sup>−1</sup>μ (adjusted
    for targets), and everything reduces to the covariance matrix Σ and its inverse — which is
    why covariance estimation <em>is</em> portfolio theory in practice: with n assets you must
    estimate n(n−1)/2 covariances (a 500-stock matrix has ~125,000 entries), and shrinkage,
    factor structure (Σ ≈ BΛB<sup>T</sup> + D) and regularisation are the standard fixes.
    <strong>Utility ties the frame to people.</strong> Markowitz's expected-utility motivation
    (with quadratic utility, mean-variance is exact; otherwise it is an approximation good for
    near-normal returns) connects to the behavioural layer: loss aversion and prospect theory
    predict different portfolio choices than the elliptical indifference curves assume, and
    drawdown-based risk measures (maximum drawdown, CVaR) capture what variance misses —
    practitioner risk is lived path-dependently, not just measured end-point.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> whether the equity risk premium is stable enough for
    long-horizon optimisation at all (its historical ~4–6% estimate carries wide error bars);
    how to optimise when the opportunity set itself adapts (crowded trades change the
    covariances being optimised on — reflexivity in the covariance matrix); what ESG screening
    does to frontiers (constraining the set shifts the frontier inward — the efficient-set
    arithmetic of values-based investing is measurable); and whether post-2022 experience
    (stocks and bonds falling together as rates spiked — correlation turning positive exactly
    when the 60/40 needed it) forces a re-estimation of the last forty years' covariance
    structure, or was one regime in a longer cycle. The 1952 identity has not changed; the
    inputs' stability — as always in finance — has.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final integration note: Markowitz's framework and the behavioural layer meet in
    practice through policy-based investing. Pre-commitment devices — fixed rebalancing
    schedules, glidepaths, default funds in auto-enrolment systems — operationalise
    mean-variance logic while insulating decisions from the loss aversion and recency effects
    the behavioural literature documents; the default-fund revolution is applied behavioural
    portfolio theory at population scale. The theory's deepest practical lesson survives
    every refinement: what you hold matters less than how the holdings move together, and
    discipline about that covariance — measured, monitored, and not chased — is the whole
    game. Everything else in this chapter is machinery for keeping that one insight honest
    when the inputs move.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: portfolio variance, CAPM, error maximisation, what diversification can't do" widget="quiz" config={mptQuiz} />

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Markowitz, Journal of Finance (1952, Nobel 1990); Tobin (1958); Sharpe, "Capital Asset Prices" (1964); Michaud, Efficient Asset Management; DeMiguel, Garlappi &amp; Uppal (2009) on 1/n; Ledoit &amp; Wolf on shrinkage; Elton &amp; Gruber, Modern Portfolio Theory.</p>
</TopicPage>
